      "writtenOn": "2022-06-10T15:01:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "For my own benefit why is it suggested to include a header rather than have a struct forward reference? In my experience avoiding unnecessary header file inclusion can improve build times so in my own code reviews I prefer struct forward references to one-off includes. But I have not done any build profiling in many years, so perhaps the benefits from years ago are no longer applicable today?",
      "parentUuid": "aeed33c5_af6af3e9",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 12,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 14,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "529803bb9c18a75cb4e11e3334cf3f407973cc5a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6e13edd0_e546b197",
        "filename": "include/trace/hooks/gic_v3.h",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 14,
      "author": {
        "id": 1625880
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-06-10T15:26:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It\u0027s suggested because if the interface is incompletely typed, we may not notice if the types change.\n\nThere are various ways we could go about doing this. Including files here is one fairly obvious approach. The design space is rather dependent on the ABI tooling we currently have or can build in the very near future.\n\nAnd, yes, this is not a theoretical problem, ABI breakage is a current issue.",
      "parentUuid": "b8c947fe_69b1076b",
